## [2.0.3] - September 2026
### Fixed
- Added `lifecycle { ignore_changes = [guest_customization, cd_roms] }` to
  `nutanix_virtual_machine_v2`. Without it the module was not idempotent and
  every plan against an existing VM proposed destroying and recreating it.

  Two separate causes, both verified against HOB-CL-DEV1:

  1. Prism Central does not return the cloud-init / Sysprep payload on read —
     `guest_customization` is write-only. Refresh therefore lost it, the block
     appeared absent from state and present in config, and because the
     attribute forces replacement, every plan produced `-/+ destroy and then
     create`.
  2. AHV automatically attaches a CONFIG_DRIVE_V2 CD-ROM
     (`iso_type = GUEST_CUSTOMIZATION`) to deliver the cloud-init payload. It
     appears on read but is never declared in config, so Terraform proposed
     removing it on every plan. Applying that would detach the config drive
     from a running VM, and every newly created VM would show the same diff on
     its first plan.

  Both are semantically create-time-only: guest customisation runs once at
  first boot, and the config drive is an artefact of it. To rebuild with a new
  payload, replace the VM explicitly:
    terraform apply -replace='module.<name>.nutanix_virtual_machine_v2.this[0]'

  This is NOT a reversal of the `ignore_changes = [categories]` removal in
  2.0.0. Categories are mutable, meaningful after creation, and drive Veeam
  job assignment — they must stay managed.

- `vm_ip` output corrected to return null rather than a `try()` chain over
  candidate attribute paths. `try()` only catches dynamic errors; a reference
  to an attribute that does not exist in the provider schema is a static error
  and would have failed `terraform validate` for every consumer. Prism Central
  only reports a learned guest IP when NGT or an equivalent agent is present,
  and the Rocky GenericCloud image does not ship NGT, so no path would have
  returned a value regardless.

### Validation
- Full lifecycle verified against HOB-CL-DEV1 on 2026-09-01: create (5m5s),
  re-plan showing no changes, category value change applied in place with the
  VM UUID unchanged, clean destroy, and re-apply with no state residue.

## [2.0.2] - August 2026
### Changed
- Raised required_version to >= 1.9 to match CCoE Terraform conventions.
- Renamed nutanix_vm.tf to mr-nutanix_vm.tf per the mr-<topic>.tf file
  naming convention. No functional change.
### Added
- nutanix_wait_timeout variable (default 10 minutes) so callers can extend
  the provider timeout for slow clusters. Nutanix VM creation on
  HOB-CL-DEV1 has been measured at over 5 minutes. The variable is
  declared here as part of the module interface; the provider block that
  consumes it lives in the calling root module.

## [2.0.1] - August 2026
### Fixed
- Category key `Umi_ITResponsible` corrected to `UMI_ITResponsible` to match
  the live Prism Central schema. Nine of the ten mandatory Umicore category
  keys use the `Umi_` prefix; this one uses uppercase `UMI_`. With the
  incorrect casing the category lookup returned no results and every plan
  failed at the ext_id index. Verified against
  GET /api/prism/v4.0/config/categories on 2026-08-21.
- Mock unit test now asserts the UMI_ITResponsible key explicitly to prevent
  the casing being normalised again.

## [2.0.0] - August 2026
### Changed — BREAKING
- Image path migrated from the v3 API resource `nutanix_virtual_machine`
  to the v4 API resource `nutanix_virtual_machine_v2`. Attribute mapping:
  `cluster_uuid` -> `cluster { ext_id }`, `num_vcpus_per_socket` ->
  `num_cores_per_socket`, `memory_size_mib` -> `memory_size_bytes`,
  `boot_device_order_list` -> `boot_config { legacy_boot { boot_order } }`,
  `disk_list` -> `disks { backing_info { vm_disk { ... } } }`, `nic_list` ->
  `nics { nic_network_info { virtual_ethernet_nic_network_info { subnet
  { ext_id } } } }`. The module's public variable interface is unchanged,
  but the resource address changes, so existing state requires
  `terraform state mv` or re-import. Released as 2.0.0 for that reason.
- Data sources migrated to v4 list endpoints: `nutanix_cluster` ->
  `nutanix_clusters_v2`, `nutanix_subnet` -> `nutanix_subnets_v2`,
  `nutanix_image` -> `nutanix_images_v2` (filtered by name via OData).
- Category keys migrated to the live Umicore Prism Central schema. All ten
  mandatory categories are now `Umi_`-prefixed (`Umi_BusinessUnit`,
  `Umi_Environment`, `Umi_Criticality`, `Umi_Recharge`,
  `Umi_PrimaryFunction`, `Umi_Application`, `Umi_Description`,
  `Umi_BUResponsible`, `Umi_ITResponsible`, `Umi_Backup`). The previous
  unprefixed names did not exist in Prism Central and failed at plan time.
- Provider constraint pinned to exactly `2.4.2`. The previous `~> 2.4`
  range could resolve to 2.4.1, which upstream marks "Invalid Release".
  2.4.2 is also the first release supporting `api_key` authentication.
- Enum validations removed from `category_environment`, `category_criticality`
  and `category_backup` in favour of non-empty checks. The hardcoded enums
  (Gold/Silver/Bronze/None; prd/tst/acc/dev; Critical/High/Medium/Low) did
  not match the live Prism Central schema and blocked every plan. Prism
  Central is now the authoritative source of valid values; an invalid value
  fails at plan time when the category lookup returns no match.

### Added
- `nutanix_categories_v2` data source lookups to resolve each mandatory
  category (and extra_tags) name/value pair to its ext_id (UUID), which the
  v4 `nutanix_virtual_machine_v2` `categories` block requires in place of the
  v3 name/value pairs. One lookup per category is issued at plan time.
- `vm_ip` output now attempts to read the learned guest IP instead of
  returning a hardcoded null. Wrapped in `try()` across candidate attribute
  paths so a wrong path degrades to null rather than failing the plan. The
  correct path is to be confirmed by the first real apply and then collapsed
  to a single expression.

### Removed
- `lifecycle { ignore_changes = [categories] }` on
  `nutanix_virtual_machine_v2`. This froze categories at creation time,
  meaning a change to `Umi_Backup` would never reach Prism Central and the
  VM would remain on its original Veeam tier indefinitely with no error.

### Known limitations
- Template path (`nutanix_deploy_templates_v2`) still cannot apply
  categories. Confirmed against the 2.4.2 provider binary:
  `override_vm_config_map` exposes no categories argument. Because
  `Umi_Backup` drives Veeam job assignment, template-deployed VMs require an
  out-of-band category step. See the remediation options documented in
  `nutanix_vm.tf`.

## [1.0.0] - June 2026
### Added
- Initial release of the nutanix_vm Terraform module
- Unified VM provisioning module supporting both template path
  (nutanix_deploy_templates_v2) and image path (nutanix_virtual_machine)
  via the source_type variable
- All 10 mandatory HLD Nutanix categories enforced as required inputs
- Dynamic naming logic: UMICORE_LOCATION-usage_code-NNNN
- vm_name_override support for acquired/migrated site legacy hostnames
- Guest customisation: Sysprep (Windows), cloud-init (Linux),
  none (appliances)
- Data disk support via data_disks variable
- Sub-resource naming for vDisks and vNICs derived from vm_name
